# Odoo Development Rules (Common)

This document defines the core development rules for building Odoo modules
in this repository.

These rules apply to all modules in the project and must be respected when
generating or modifying code.

They are designed to ensure:

- consistency of the codebase
- maintainable architecture
- compatibility with Odoo 19
- safe AI-assisted development


--------------------------------------------------
General Principles
--------------------------------------------------

Prefer simple and explicit implementations.

Avoid introducing unnecessary complexity.

Favor readability and maintainability over clever solutions.

When multiple approaches are possible, prefer the one that:

- uses the Odoo ORM
- keeps business logic inside models
- minimizes side effects.


--------------------------------------------------
Language and Naming
--------------------------------------------------

All code must be written in English.

This includes:

- model names
- field names
- method names
- comments
- docstrings
- UI labels.

Model technical names should follow the pattern:

```
module_name.model_name
```

Examples:

```
me.document_exp
me.document_movement
```

Field names should be descriptive and consistent.


--------------------------------------------------
Model Design
--------------------------------------------------

Models must represent real domain entities.

Avoid introducing new models unless the concept:

- has its own lifecycle
- represents a distinct entity
- cannot be represented by extending an existing model.

Before proposing a new model, consult:

```
docs/model_registry.md
```


--------------------------------------------------
Model Responsibilities
--------------------------------------------------

Each model should have a clear responsibility.

Avoid models that mix unrelated responsibilities.

Prefer extending existing models rather than duplicating data structures.


--------------------------------------------------
Business Logic Placement
--------------------------------------------------

Business logic must be implemented in Python models.

Views should only define the user interface.

Never implement business rules in XML views.


--------------------------------------------------
ORM Usage
--------------------------------------------------

Always prefer the Odoo ORM.

Avoid direct SQL queries unless strictly necessary.

If SQL is required:

- document the reason
- ensure the query is safe.


--------------------------------------------------
Method Overrides
--------------------------------------------------

When overriding core methods such as:

```
create
write
unlink
```

always call the parent implementation unless there is a strong reason not to.


Example:

```
record = super().create(vals)
```

Avoid complex side effects in overrides unless required by business logic.


--------------------------------------------------
Compute Fields
--------------------------------------------------

Compute methods must be deterministic and side-effect free.

Compute methods must not:

- create records
- modify unrelated models
- send notifications
- perform heavy operations.

Use `store=True` when the value is frequently accessed.


--------------------------------------------------
Onchange Usage
--------------------------------------------------

`@api.onchange` should only be used for UI behavior.

Business logic must not depend on onchange methods.


--------------------------------------------------
Relationships
--------------------------------------------------

Use proper relational fields:

```
Many2one
One2many
Many2many
```

Avoid storing redundant data when it can be obtained through relations.


--------------------------------------------------
Constraints
--------------------------------------------------

Use constraints to enforce data integrity.

Prefer:

```
@api.constrains
```

over manual validation scattered across methods.


--------------------------------------------------
Security
--------------------------------------------------

Every model must define explicit access rules.

Access control should be defined in:

```
security/ir.model.access.csv
```

Use security groups when different roles require different access levels.


--------------------------------------------------
Views
--------------------------------------------------

Views must remain declarative.

Avoid embedding business logic in view definitions.

Views should focus on:

- layout
- field visibility
- navigation.


--------------------------------------------------
Code Structure
--------------------------------------------------

A typical module should follow this structure:

```
module/
    models/
    views/
    security/
    wizards/
    data/
```

Each model should live in its own Python file when possible.


--------------------------------------------------
Documentation
--------------------------------------------------

If a change affects the system structure, update:

```
docs/models.md
docs/rules_business.md
docs/model_registry.md
```

Documentation must remain consistent with the code.


--------------------------------------------------
AI-Assisted Development
--------------------------------------------------

When generating code with AI:

1. Analyze existing models before introducing new ones.
2. Respect the current architecture.
3. Avoid unnecessary refactors.
4. Prefer extending existing models.

If behavior is unclear in the existing codebase,
mark it as:

```
pending definition
```

instead of inventing functionality.


--------------------------------------------------
System Integrity
--------------------------------------------------

The following principles must always hold:

- models represent domain entities
- business logic lives in models
- views define UI only
- documentation reflects the current architecture.

Any change that violates these principles must be reconsidered.